From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Meridian <meridian@localhost>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 14:50:19 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] Meridian: Windows: npm's claude.cmd and codex.cmd are found
 and run, and an account's variables reach the CLI

Claude Code and Codex installed with npm on Windows are claude.cmd and
codex.cmd in %APPDATA%\npm (no .exe): Meridian looked for .exe only, so
for many people it found neither. FindProgram now takes name.cmd where
there is no name.exe, and %APPDATA%\npm is looked in as
%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin is (not in tests).

A .cmd is a batch file: CreateProcess doesn't start it with arguments of
its own. WindowsCommandLine() puts it behind cmd.exe /d /s /c the way npm's
own tools (cross-spawn) do: every argument quoted for the program, then the
command prompt's special characters behind carets twice (the line is read
once, and the wrapper's %* reads the arguments again), so & | < > % ! ^ ( )
and quotes arrive as they were. RunProcess (Ask, Improve, versions) and the
Workbench's terminals use it; a unit test runs a real .cmd and reads its
arguments back.

Connect for an account ran the CLI without the account's variables on
Windows ("macOS and Linux only for now"), so Connect for Work wrote into the
default account. RunCli now hands Meridian's environment with the account's
variables (an empty one taken away) to the program itself, no shell, and
takes its exit code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
